#!/usr/bin/env bash
#MISE description="Gate: CI spends nothing an agent could have spent for free — no job on a draft, every PR workflow supersedes its own runs, and every task CI runs is one `verify` runs"
#
# CI minutes are metered; this sandbox is not. The workflow contract already
# promises that "CI runs the same `mise` tasks you run locally; one it runs that
# you can't is a bug" — and promised it in prose only, which is feedforward with
# no sensor. These properties make that promise true, and each is
# computable over the committed text alone: no network, no run history, no
# judgement. The count is not restated anywhere else, because it went stale
# twice while the list below grew.
#
#   1. NO JOB RUNS ON A DRAFT. Draft means "still being verified locally", and
#      it is also the lever a red run pulls: `land` re-drafts the PR so nothing
#      further is spent while the failure is diagnosed. A single job missing the
#      guard defeats both. `zizmor.yml` was that job — it triggers on any PR
#      touching a workflow, so every push to a draft that edited one spent a
#      runner, and re-drafting did not close the tap (CLOUD-240).
#
#   2. EVERY PR WORKFLOW SUPERSEDES ITS OWN RUNS. A landing lap rebases and
#      pushes; without `cancel-in-progress` the superseded SHA's run is paid out
#      in full for a verdict nobody reads. `ci.yml` had this; the other two did
#      not — which is also what lets `land` cancel a doomed run by simply
#      pushing the next one, rather than calling `gh run cancel`.
#
#   3. EVERY TASK CI RUNS IS ONE `verify` RUNS. Otherwise CI is where a failure
#      is DISCOVERED rather than confirmed, and the discovery costs a runner and
#      a round trip that a free local run would have caught.
#
#   4. THE RELEASE PR IS A DRAFT UNTIL THE DEBOUNCE READIES IT. release-plz
#      rewrites the `release-plz-*` branch on every push to `main`, and a
#      non-draft PR turns each of those refreshes into a full matrix on a diff
#      that is a version bump and a changelog entry — 5 of 30 `ci.yml` runs in
#      one measured 37-minute window, four of them thrown away by the next
#      refresh (CLOUD-346). `pr_draft` is the whole mechanism, so it is the
#      thing to sense: property 1 gates every job on the draft flag, and this
#      gates the flag itself being set.
#
#   5. THE REQUIRED SET NAMES EXACTLY THESE JOBS. `ci-wait` and `land` decide
#      "did this SHA get an answer" over `$CI_REQUIRED_CHECKS` (CLOUD-327), a
#      hand-maintained list until CLOUD-54's derived `[ci].required_checks`
#      lands. A hand-maintained list is only safe with a sensor on it: a job
#      added and not listed is silently unrequired — the exact false green
#      CLOUD-327 is — and a listed name matching no job waits forever for a run
#      nothing will ever create. Both directions, over the committed text.
#
#   6. A FAN-IN ASSERTS ITS WHOLE `needs:` SET. Branch protection points at one
#      aggregating job so that adding a leg never needs a ruleset change — which
#      only holds if that job's assertion follows `needs:` by itself. `final`
#      enumerated three of its four dependencies, so a red `msrv` left green the
#      one check the host requires (CLOUD-351).
#
#   7. AN UNAUTHORISED RUN IS STOPPED BEFORE IT SPENDS. The landing lease
#      (CLOUD-393) serialises landing, but it was enforced entirely inside
#      `mise-tasks/land` — so anything else pushing to a ready PR bought a full
#      matrix without ever touching the lock. Measured 2026-08-12: four
#      concurrent matrices while the lease changed hands three times, every
#      holder honouring it. The remedy is a first step in every job that can
#      start immediately, and this is the sensor that no job is added without
#      it. Jobs with `needs:` are exempt, and exempt for a REASON rather than by
#      enumeration: a fan-in cannot start before its dependencies are terminal,
#      so it can never spend a runner ahead of the cancellation.
#
#   8. EVERY WORKFLOW DECLARES A CONCURRENCY GROUP. Property 2 is the landing
#      path's half of this and was the whole of it, which is why eleven
#      workflows had none: it is guarded on `pull_request`, and
#      `fast-forward.yml` is `issue_comment` while the scheduled ones avoid
#      `pull_request` on purpose (see the scoping note below). So the property
#      that matters off the landing path — that a workflow cannot have two runs
#      racing at all — reached none of them. `fast-forward.yml` is the one that
#      cost: N concurrent `/fast-forward` comments ran N concurrent attempts to
#      advance `main`, measured as 245 refusals against 6 merges in a half hour
#      (CLOUD-393). The two arms are NOT one: a PR workflow supersedes itself
#      (`cancel-in-progress: true`), a scheduled one must NOT be killed by its
#      own next tick, so declaring a group is all this asks of it.
#
#   9. NO TWO SCHEDULES COLLIDE. Every scheduled workflow's header claims a
#      staggered slot and nothing checked, so two pairs drifted onto the same
#      minute — and the second pair landed AFTER the first was found. Compared
#      as LITERAL cron expressions, never as firing times: `auto-release-land`
#      runs `*/30 * * * *`, which genuinely overlaps every `:00` and `:30` slot,
#      and flagging that would make the property fire forever on a workflow
#      doing nothing wrong. Literal equality is also what "a staggered slot"
#      means in the headers making the claim.
#
#  10. A `workflow_run` TRIGGER FILTERS WHERE FILTERING IS FREE. A job `if:` is
#      evaluated after the run already exists, so a branch scope expressed only
#      there creates a run and then skips it. `auto-dependabot-land` did that
#      1131 times in 25 hours and skipped 1131 — no runner minutes, which is why
#      it survived, but 46% of every run in the repository, and at ~3100
#      inserted runs a day the global run list shifts page boundaries mid-walk,
#      so paginating it is not stable. Keyed to the `if:` rather than demanded of
#      every `workflow_run` trigger: without that, the gate cannot tell a
#      deliberately repository-wide trigger from one that meant to be narrow.
#
#  11. A WORKFLOW THAT READS CHECK STATUS DECIDES GREEN THROUGH `checks-green`.
#      The predicate has one home, and every hand-rolled copy of it so far has
#      been the same defect: `select(.conclusion != "skipped")` counts a wholly
#      skipped set as zero outstanding, i.e. green, which is what a draft-era
#      refresh looks like — CLOUD-327's false green, and #261 landed on exactly
#      that set. It survived on the Dependabot lane for as long as it did because
#      nothing sensed it: `tests/ci-local-parity.bats` reaches `pull_request`
#      workflows and an auto-lander is `workflow_run` (CLOUD-391). Keyed to the
#      check-runs ENDPOINT rather than to a banned jq spelling, because the
#      spelling is what a rewrite changes and the fetch is what it cannot avoid;
#      the copy is only reachable by reading that endpoint yourself.
#
# Properties 1-7 are scoped to `pull_request`-triggered workflows: a scheduled or
# release workflow is not on the landing path and they do not apply to it. 8, 9,
# 10 and 11 are the exceptions — a workflow racing itself, colliding with its
# neighbour, buying a run it will only skip, or landing a SHA on a predicate of
# its own are none of them properties of the landing path — so they judge every
# workflow, and 8 and 10 sit before the `pull_request` filter for that reason.
set -uo pipefail

workflows="${PARITY_WORKFLOWS:-.github/workflows}"
manifest="${PARITY_MANIFEST:-mise.toml}"
release_plz="${PARITY_RELEASE_PLZ:-release-plz.toml}"
fail=0

problem() {
	echo "::error:: ci-local-parity: $*" >&2
	fail=1
}

# What `mise run verify` actually runs: `[tasks.verify]`'s block AND
# `[tasks."verify:gated"]`'s, `depends` lists and bodies alike. A task named in
# any of them is one an agent runs locally before readying.
#
# TWO blocks since CLOUD-407, and reading only the first is why this gate is
# named here rather than left to infer. `verify` is now a dependency-free mapper
# owning the exit-code contract — 2 means "main moved", every other failure is a
# stop — and the whole gate set moved to `verify:gated` so that no `depends`
# code can escape the mapping. The tasks did not stop being run; they moved one
# hop. A reader that follows only the first hop reports every one of them as
# CI-only, which is a false alarm rather than a missed one, so this fails in the
# safe direction — but it fails on every commit, which is its own defect.
#
# The hop is spelled out rather than chased generically. `verify` names its
# successor in exactly one place, a third link would break this loudly (a CI task
# found in no block is a `problem`), and an awk that followed `mise run` calls
# transitively would be a second authority on the task graph mise already owns.
verify_block=$(awk '/^\[tasks\.verify\]|^\[tasks\."verify:gated"\]/{p=1;next} /^\[/{p=0} p' "$manifest")
if [ -z "$verify_block" ]; then
	problem "no [tasks.verify] in $manifest, so there is nothing to compare CI against."
	exit 1
fi

# The required set, read from the same manifest `[tasks.verify]` came from —
# not from the environment, so a fixture manifest exercises the property and the
# real one is what the last bats case judges.
required=$(awk -F'"' '/^CI_REQUIRED_CHECKS = /{print $2; exit}' "$manifest")
[ -n "$required" ] ||
	problem "no CI_REQUIRED_CHECKS in $manifest, so \`ci-wait\` has no required set and every check is unrequired."

# A check-run's name is the job's `name:`; a matrix leg gets the leg appended in
# parentheses, which no committed text can expand. Comparison is therefore over
# the base name — enough to catch a job added, removed or renamed, which is what
# rots a list. Job `name:` sits at exactly four spaces; a step's `- name:` is
# deeper and stays out.
base_names() { sed -E 's/[[:space:]]*\(.*$//' | sed -E 's/[[:space:]]+$//' | sort -u; }
want_names=$(printf '%s\n' "$required" | tr ',' '\n' | base_names)
job_names=""

checked=0
all_seen=0
for wf in "$workflows"/*.yml; do
	[ -e "$wf" ] || continue
	# The trigger list is the two-space-indented block under `on:`. Read into a
	# variable first: `grep -q` exits on the first match, and under `pipefail` a
	# producer killed by that SIGPIPE makes the pipeline report failure on a
	# MATCH — the inversion `pipefail-grep-check` exists to catch.
	triggers=$(awk '/^on:/{p=1;next} /^[a-z]/{p=0} p' "$wf")

	# Property 8, and it sits HERE — above the filter — on purpose: a workflow
	# racing itself is not a property of the landing path, so scoping it to
	# `pull_request` is what left eleven workflows unjudged. Only the group's
	# existence is asked for; `cancel-in-progress` is property 2's arm below,
	# because the right value differs by trigger and a single answer is wrong
	# for one of them.
	all_seen=$((all_seen + 1))
	grep -q '^concurrency:' "$wf" ||
		problem "$wf declares no concurrency group, so two of its runs can race — add one, with cancel-in-progress: true if it triggers on pull_request and false otherwise."

	# Property 11, above the filter for the same reason as 8 and 10: a value
	# that never reaches GitHub is not a property of the landing path.
	#
	# YAML opens a comment at an unquoted ` #`, so
	# `run-name: fast-forward #${{ github.event.issue.number }}` parses to the
	# bare string `fast-forward` and the interpolation is discarded. Measured on
	# this repo (CLOUD-507): `fast-forward.yml` carried exactly that line for a
	# day, every one of 30 consecutive runs reported `display_title` equal to the
	# workflow NAME, and `land`'s `select(.display_title == env.FF_KEY)` could
	# therefore never match — restoring CLOUD-235's "cannot see a refusal, so it
	# polls forever" while the success path masked it. `actionlint` and `zizmor`
	# both pass over the line, because a comment is legal YAML, and review reads
	# it as the thing it was meant to be.
	#
	# ANCHORED, NEVER COUNTED, and the difference is measured rather than
	# asserted. The obvious predicate — compare the raw `${{` count against the
	# count surviving a YAML parse — flags 4 of this repo's 20 workflows, and 3
	# are `nonverdict-rate`, `release-assets` and `timeout-drift` legitimately
	# discussing `${{ }}` inside prose comments. A gate that is 75% false
	# positives gets switched off, which is worse than no gate. Requiring a
	# `key:` before the `#` and an interpolation after it, with whole-line
	# comments skipped, flags exactly the one real defect and nothing else.
	#
	# Two greps and a here-string rather than a pipe: `pipefail-grep-check`
	# refuses a producer piped into an early-exiting grep, and capturing first
	# keeps this out of that shape entirely.
	# The first value character must not be a quote. A quoted scalar carries its
	# `#` as data — that is the REPAIR this rule asks for, so matching it would
	# make the gate refuse the very shape it demands. Found by the gate refusing
	# its own fix on the first run, which is the cheapest possible way to find it.
	swallowed_raw=$(grep -nE '^[[:space:]]*[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+:[[:space:]]*[^"'"'"'#[:space:]][^#]*[[:space:]]#.*\$\{\{' "$wf" || true)
	swallowed=$(grep -vE '^[0-9]+:[[:space:]]*#' <<<"$swallowed_raw" || true)
	while IFS= read -r hit; do
		[ -n "$hit" ] || continue
		# Pointer-only (non-negotiable 4): the path, the line, and the repair.
		# Never the line's text — a workflow value can name a secret.
		problem "$wf:${hit%%:*} has an unquoted '#' inside a key's value, which opens a YAML comment and swallows a \${{ }} interpolation — the value reaching GitHub is truncated. Quote it (CLOUD-507)."
	done <<<"$swallowed"

	# Property 10, also above the filter: a `workflow_run` workflow is not on the
	# landing path, and this is about what it COSTS the run list. Keyed to the
	# job condition rather than demanded of every `workflow_run` trigger —
	# otherwise the gate cannot tell a deliberately repository-wide trigger from
	# one that meant to be narrow and said so in the wrong place. Here-strings
	# and a direct file read, never a pipe into `grep -q`, for the `pipefail`
	# reason above.
	if grep -q '^  workflow_run:' <<<"$triggers" &&
		grep -q 'workflow_run\.head_branch' "$wf" &&
		! grep -q '^    branches:' <<<"$triggers"; then
		problem "$wf filters on workflow_run.head_branch in a job condition but its trigger declares no branches: filter — a job \`if:\` is evaluated after the run already exists, so every non-matching completion still creates a run and then skips it."
	fi

	grep -q '^  pull_request:' <<<"$triggers" || continue
	checked=$((checked + 1))

	wf_job_names=$(awk '
		/^jobs:/ { injobs = 1; next }
		injobs && /^[a-z]/ { injobs = 0 }
		injobs && /^    name:[[:space:]]/ { sub(/^    name:[[:space:]]*/, ""); print }
	' "$wf")
	job_names="$job_names
$wf_job_names"

	# Property 11. A REQUIRED CHECK MUST BE ABLE TO BE SUPERSEDED (CLOUD-503).
	#
	# Omitting `types:` defaults to `[opened, synchronize, reopened]`. Every job
	# here is draft-gated — property 1 above makes that universal — so a PR
	# created as a draft mints a `skipped` run on `opened`, and with no
	# `ready_for_review` there is no event left that could replace it. `land`
	# readies before pushing and its push moves nothing whenever HEAD is already
	# on the remote, so the ready event is the only one that can arrive;
	# `checks-green` correctly refuses to read a skip as an answer, and the poll
	# never terminates while the branch holds the fleet lease.
	#
	# Measured as a deadlock across two PRs at once, both fully green but for one
	# such name, with `main` frozen so neither could rebase out of it.
	#
	# SCOPED TO WORKFLOWS PRODUCING A REQUIRED NAME, deliberately. A workflow
	# whose checks nothing waits on may miss the ready event with no consequence,
	# and demanding the key of every `pull_request` workflow would be a gate
	# asserting more than the failure needs — the reason this sits beside the
	# job-name collection rather than in the `want_names` comparison at the end,
	# which has the names but no longer knows which file they came from.
	produces_required=0
	while IFS= read -r job_name; do
		[ -n "$job_name" ] || continue
		if grep -qxF "$job_name" <<<"$want_names"; then
			produces_required=1
			break
		fi
	done < <(printf '%s\n' "$wf_job_names" | sed '/^$/d' | base_names)
	if [ "$produces_required" = 1 ]; then
		# The `pull_request:` sub-block only. A `types:` belonging to another
		# trigger says nothing about this one, and reading the whole `on:` block
		# would let one satisfy the other.
		pr_block=$(awk '/^  pull_request:/{p=1;next} /^  [A-Za-z_]+:/{p=0} p' <<<"$triggers")
		grep -q 'ready_for_review' <<<"$pr_block" ||
			problem "$wf produces a required check but its pull_request trigger omits \`ready_for_review\` — the draft-era \`opened\` run is a skip nothing can then supersede, so \`ci-wait\` polls it forever while the lease is held. Add: types: [opened, synchronize, reopened, ready_for_review] (CLOUD-503)."
	fi

	grep -q 'cancel-in-progress: true' "$wf" ||
		problem "$wf triggers on pull_request but never supersedes its own runs — add a concurrency group with cancel-in-progress: true, or a lap pays out the run it just made obsolete."

	# Every job's block, from its two-space key to the next one, must carry the
	# draft guard somewhere in it.
	ungated=$(awk '
		/^jobs:/ { injobs = 1; next }
		injobs && /^[a-z]/ { injobs = 0 }
		injobs && /^  [A-Za-z0-9_-]+:[[:space:]]*$/ {
			if (job != "" && !guarded) print job
			job = $1; sub(":", "", job); guarded = 0
		}
		injobs && /github\.event\.pull_request\.draft == false/ { guarded = 1 }
		END { if (job != "" && !guarded) print job }
	' "$wf")
	if [ -n "$ungated" ]; then
		while IFS= read -r job; do
			problem "$wf job '$job' runs on a draft PR — add: if: \${{ github.event.pull_request.draft == false }}"
		done <<<"$ungated"
	fi

	# A fan-in job must assert over its WHOLE `needs:` set. `needs:` is the real
	# dependency list; an assertion enumerating it by hand is a second authority
	# for the same fact, and only the second one decides the verdict. `final`
	# carried exactly that: four dependencies, three asserted, so a red `msrv`
	# left green the one check branch protection requires (CLOUD-351).
	#
	# Satisfied EITHER by a set-wide predicate (`needs.*`, which cannot go stale
	# because it names nothing) OR by naming every dependency. Both spellings of
	# a name count — `needs.darwin-link` and `needs['darwin-link']` — since a key
	# with a hyphen is legal in both.
	unasserted=$(awk '
		function flush(   i, n, parts, miss) {
			if (job == "" || needs == "") return
			if (index(body, "needs.*")) return
			n = split(needs, parts, /[ ,]+/); miss = ""
			for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
				if (parts[i] == "") continue
				if (index(body, "needs." parts[i])) continue
				if (index(body, "needs[\x27" parts[i] "\x27]")) continue
				miss = miss (miss == "" ? "" : ",") parts[i]
			}
			if (miss != "") print job "\t" miss
		}
		/^jobs:/ { injobs = 1; next }
		injobs && /^[a-z]/ { flush(); injobs = 0 }
		injobs && /^  [A-Za-z0-9_-]+:[[:space:]]*$/ {
			flush()
			job = $1; sub(":", "", job); needs = ""; body = ""; inneeds = 0
			next
		}
		injobs && /^    needs:[[:space:]]*\[/ {
			line = $0; sub(/^[^[]*\[/, "", line); sub(/\].*$/, "", line)
			gsub(/["\x27]/, "", line); needs = line; inneeds = 0; next
		}
		injobs && /^    needs:[[:space:]]*$/ { inneeds = 1; next }
		injobs && inneeds && /^      -[[:space:]]/ {
			line = $0; sub(/^      -[[:space:]]*/, "", line)
			gsub(/["\x27]/, "", line); needs = needs " " line; next
		}
		injobs && inneeds && /^    [A-Za-z]/ { inneeds = 0 }
		injobs { body = body "\n" $0 }
		END { flush() }
	' "$wf")
	if [ -n "$unasserted" ]; then
		while IFS=$'\t' read -r job missing; do
			[ -n "$job" ] || continue
			problem "$wf job '$job' waits on '$missing' but never asserts it — a fan-in that enumerates its own needs: goes stale the next time one is added (CLOUD-351). Assert over needs.* instead."
		done <<<"$unasserted"
	fi

	# Property 7. The precondition must be FIRST — a job that installs a
	# toolchain and then asks permission has already spent most of what asking
	# was meant to save. Matched by the step's `name:`, not its body: seven
	# copies of a ten-line body would be seven things to keep in step, which is
	# why the body lives in `mise-tasks/ci-lease-precondition` and the workflows
	# carry only the fetch.
	unguarded=$(awk '
		function flush() {
			if (job != "" && !hasneeds && !guarded) print job
		}
		/^jobs:/ { injobs = 1; next }
		injobs && /^[a-z]/ { flush(); injobs = 0 }
		injobs && /^  [A-Za-z0-9_-]+:[[:space:]]*$/ {
			flush()
			job = $1; sub(":", "", job)
			hasneeds = 0; guarded = 0; insteps = 0; seen = 0
			next
		}
		injobs && /^    needs:/ { hasneeds = 1 }
		injobs && /^    steps:[[:space:]]*$/ { insteps = 1; next }
		injobs && insteps && /^      -[[:space:]]/ {
			if (!seen) {
				seen = 1
				if (index($0, "name: Landing lease precondition")) guarded = 1
			}
		}
		END { flush() }
	' "$wf")
	if [ -n "$unguarded" ]; then
		while IFS= read -r job; do
			problem "$wf job '$job' starts without asking whether the landing lease authorises this branch — add \`- name: Landing lease precondition\` as its first step, or it buys a full matrix while another branch is landing (CLOUD-420)."
		done <<<"$unguarded"
	fi

	# AND THE STEP MUST BE ALLOWED TO FAIL. The property above matches the step's
	# NAME, so a copy that reds its own job reads as present and correct. The
	# precondition's own header promises it "NEVER EXITS NON-ZERO", and its suite
	# calls that the property that outranks every row — but the promise is made by
	# the fetched program, and the caller is what decides whether a body that will
	# not PARSE is fatal. A truncated response, a proxy error page returned 200, or
	# a bad edit to the trunk copy makes `bash -c` a syntax error in the FIRST step
	# of every job: the step reds under the runner's default `bash -e {0}`, `final`
	# fails its `needs:` under `always()`, and `land` re-drafts on red — so one bad
	# response on `main` re-drafts the whole fleet through the mechanism built to
	# protect it. Counted rather than grepped for absence, because the two forms
	# differ only by a suffix and a `grep -v` would pass a file carrying both.
	# shellcheck disable=SC2016  # `$body` is the workflow's text being searched for, not this script's variable
	invocations=$(grep -c 'bash -c "\$body"' "$wf" || true)
	# shellcheck disable=SC2016  # ditto — the pattern is the YAML's own `bash -c "$body" || exit 0`
	tolerant=$(grep -c 'bash -c "\$body" || exit 0' "$wf" || true)
	[ "$invocations" = "$tolerant" ] ||
		problem "$wf runs the fetched precondition body without \`|| exit 0\` — a body that will not parse must not red the first step of every job (CLOUD-420)."

	# `mise run <task>` in the STEPS, not in the prose. These files carry long
	# comments that name tasks to explain why they are absent, and a plain grep
	# read those as spend — a gate that fires on its own documentation is a gate
	# people delete. So: `run:` scalars and the bodies of `run: |` blocks only.
	# The task name only, too; trailing arguments (a matrix target, say) are the
	# job's business, not this gate's.
	#
	# AND ONLY FROM A JOB THIS MACHINE COULD HAVE RUN (CLOUD-394). The property's
	# premise is its own header's: "a free local run would have caught it". That
	# is false for a job on an OS the agent is not — there is no local Windows —
	# so applied there it is not a parity check but a prohibition, and it forbade
	# any cross-OS job outright. CLOUD-113 is the instance: a Windows test job
	# passed all five other properties and could not be committed at all.
	#
	# The exemption is an ALLOWLIST OF FOREIGN LABELS, not an allowlist of Linux
	# ones, and the direction is the whole correctness of it. Exempting anything
	# that is not `ubuntu-*` would also exempt `self-hosted`, a matrix
	# expression, and any label nobody has classified — silently switching the
	# property off for jobs it should judge. So: exempt only what is KNOWN to be
	# a different OS, judge everything else. `${{ matrix.os }}` is judged, which
	# is the honest verdict for a job that may well include a Linux leg. Same
	# fail-closed shape as `lock-complete`'s backend exemption.
	#
	# Buffered per job and decided at flush, never as the lines are read: `runs-on`
	# conventionally precedes `steps:` but nothing requires it, and a gate that
	# depended on key order would judge a job correctly or not depending on how
	# its author happened to type it.
	steps=$(awk '
		function flush() {
			if (job != "" && judged) printf "%s", buf
			buf = ""
		}
		/^jobs:/ { injobs = 1; next }
		injobs && /^[a-z]/ { flush(); injobs = 0 }
		injobs && /^  [A-Za-z0-9_-]+:[[:space:]]*$/ {
			flush()
			job = $1; sub(":", "", job)
			judged = 1; inblock = 0
			next
		}
		injobs && /^[[:space:]]+runs-on:/ {
			runner = $0
			sub(/^[[:space:]]*runs-on:[[:space:]]*/, "", runner)
			gsub(/["\x27]/, "", runner)
			if (runner ~ /^(windows|macos)-/) judged = 0
		}
		injobs && inblock && index($0, indent) != 1 && NF { inblock = 0 }
		injobs && inblock { buf = buf $0 "\n"; next }
		injobs && /^[[:space:]]*-?[[:space:]]*run:[[:space:]]*[|>]/ {
			match($0, /^[[:space:]]*/)
			indent = substr($0, 1, RLENGTH) "  "
			inblock = 1; next
		}
		injobs && /^[[:space:]]*-?[[:space:]]*run:/ { buf = buf $0 "\n" }
		END { flush() }
	' "$wf")
	while IFS= read -r task; do
		[ -n "$task" ] || continue
		grep -q -- "$task" <<<"$verify_block" ||
			problem "$wf runs \`mise run $task\`, which \`mise run verify\` does not — CI would be where that failure is discovered, and discovery costs a runner."
	done < <(printf '%s\n' "$steps" | grep -oE 'mise run [a-z][a-z0-9:_-]*' | awk '{print $3}' | sort -u)
done

if [ "$checked" -eq 0 ]; then
	problem "no pull_request-triggered workflow found under $workflows — either the path is wrong or the landing path is untested."
fi

# Properties 8 and 9 judge every workflow, so they need their own "did I look at
# anything" guard: a directory holding only scheduled workflows leaves `checked`
# at zero above while these two did real work, and reusing that counter would
# report a wrong reason for a right refusal.
if [ "$all_seen" -eq 0 ]; then
	problem "no workflow found under $workflows at all — the path is wrong, and every property here silently judged nothing."
fi

# Property 9. A schedule line is `- cron: "<expr>"` under `schedule:`; a comment
# cannot match, because the anchor requires the dash before the key. Quotes are
# stripped so a file quoting differently cannot hide a collision, and the
# comparison is over the LITERAL expression — see the header for why firing-time
# overlap is deliberately not what this asks.
crons=$(for wf in "$workflows"/*.yml; do
	[ -e "$wf" ] || continue
	awk -v f="$wf" '
		/^[[:space:]]*-[[:space:]]*cron:/ {
			sub(/^[^:]*:[[:space:]]*/, "")
			gsub(/["'"'"']/, "")
			sub(/[[:space:]]+$/, "")
			if ($0 != "") print $0 "\t" f
		}
	' "$wf"
done)
while IFS= read -r expr; do
	[ -n "$expr" ] || continue
	# Pointer-only: the expression and the file names, never the file bodies.
	files=$(printf '%s\n' "$crons" | awk -F'\t' -v e="$expr" '$1 == e { n = split($2, p, "/"); printf "%s ", p[n] }')
	problem "cron \"$expr\" is declared by more than one workflow (${files% }) — they contend for the same runners at the same minute, and every one of these headers claims a staggered slot. Move one to a free minute."
done < <(printf '%s\n' "$crons" | sed '/^$/d' | cut -f1 | sort | uniq -d)

# Property 4. Read as committed text, like everything else here: the key set to
# anything but `true` is the same defect as the key absent, so match the value.
if [ ! -f "$release_plz" ]; then
	problem "no $release_plz, so nothing declares whether the release PR is a draft — every refresh of it would buy a full matrix (CLOUD-346)."
elif ! grep -Eq '^[[:space:]]*pr_draft[[:space:]]*=[[:space:]]*true[[:space:]]*$' "$release_plz"; then
	problem "$release_plz does not set \`pr_draft = true\` — release-plz would open the release PR ready, and every push to main would spend a matrix on it before the debounce is ever consulted (CLOUD-346)."
fi

# Property 11. Judged over the whole workflow set rather than the landing path,
# because a workflow that lands a SHA is not itself on `pull_request`. Comment
# lines are stripped first: this file's own remedy is described in prose in both
# auto-landers, and a property that fired on the paragraph explaining it would be
# unfixable except by deleting the explanation.
for wf in "$workflows"/*.yml; do
	[ -e "$wf" ] || continue
	body=$(sed 's/[[:space:]]*#.*$//' "$wf")
	grep -q 'check-runs' <<<"$body" || continue
	grep -q 'checks-green' <<<"$body" ||
		problem "$wf reads the check-runs endpoint but never calls \`mise run checks-green\` — the green predicate has one home, and every hand-rolled copy of it so far counted a wholly skipped set as green (CLOUD-327, CLOUD-391)."
done

have_names=$(printf '%s\n' "$job_names" | sed '/^$/d' | base_names)
while IFS= read -r name; do
	[ -n "$name" ] || continue
	grep -qxF "$name" <<<"$have_names" ||
		problem "CI_REQUIRED_CHECKS names '$name', which is no job in a pull_request workflow — \`ci-wait\` would wait for a run nothing creates."
done <<<"$want_names"
while IFS= read -r name; do
	[ -n "$name" ] || continue
	grep -qxF "$name" <<<"$want_names" ||
		problem "job '$name' runs on pull_request but is missing from CI_REQUIRED_CHECKS — \`ci-wait\` would report green without it (CLOUD-327)."
done <<<"$have_names"

[ "$fail" -eq 0 ] || exit 1
echo "ci-local-parity: $checked pull_request workflow(s) — draft-gated, self-superseding, lease-gated before they spend, running nothing verify does not, asserting every needs: they wait on, named in full by CI_REQUIRED_CHECKS, and subscribing to ready_for_review wherever a required check depends on it; the release PR opens as a draft; all $all_seen workflow(s) declare a concurrency group, decide green through checks-green wherever they read check status, and no two share a cron expression"
